Antumbra: do not set SO_REUSEADDR on tor's UDP listener (DNSPort).

On Linux, two UDP sockets that both set SO_REUSEADDR may bind the same
address and port, whatever their uids, and the one bound last receives the
datagrams. tor sets SO_REUSEADDR on every listener, so while tor holds
DNSPort 127.0.0.1:9053 any app on the phone could bind it too and receive
the DNS filter's queries (every name the phone looks up). Without the flag
on tor's socket, such a bind fails with EADDRINUSE. TCP listeners keep the
flag: they need it to rebind after TIME_WAIT, and Linux does not let another
socket bind a port that a TCP socket is listening on (SO_REUSEPORT under the
same uid aside, which tor does not set).

Checked by hostcheck-tor.sh ("cannot share ... while tor holds it").

--- a/src/core/mainloop/connection.c
+++ b/src/core/mainloop/connection.c
@@ -1537,7 +1537,12 @@
       goto err;
     }
 
-    if (make_socket_reuseable(s) < 0) {
+    /* Antumbra: TCP listeners only. On Linux, SO_REUSEADDR on a UDP socket
+     * lets any other process, under any uid, bind the same address and
+     * port while tor holds it, and take the datagrams sent to it (the DNS
+     * filter's queries to DNSPort). UDP has no TIME_WAIT for it to help
+     * with. */
+    if (is_stream && make_socket_reuseable(s) < 0) {
       log_warn(LD_NET, "Error setting SO_REUSEADDR flag on %s: %s",
                conn_type_to_string(type),
                tor_socket_strerror(errno));
